{
  "sn35.229:0.1": "Linked Discourses 35.229 ",
  "sn35.229:0.2": "18. The Ocean ",
  "sn35.229:0.3": "The Ocean (2nd) ",
  "sn35.229:1.1": "“Mendicants, an unlearned ordinary person speaks of the ocean. ",
  "sn35.229:1.2": "But that’s not the ocean in the training of the Noble One. ",
  "sn35.229:1.3": "That’s just a large body of water, a large sea of water. ",
  "sn35.229:1.4": "There are sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. ",
  "sn35.229:1.5": "This is called the ocean in the training of the Noble One. ",
  "sn35.229:1.6": "And it’s here that this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, gods and humans—is for the most part sunk. It’s become tangled like string, knotted like a ball of thread, and matted like rushes and reeds, and it doesn’t escape the places of loss, the bad places, the underworld, transmigration. ",
  "sn35.229:2.1": "There are sounds … smells … tastes … touches … ideas known by the mind, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. ",
  "sn35.229:2.2": "This is called the ocean in the training of the Noble One. ",
  "sn35.229:2.3": "And it’s here that this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, gods and humans—is for the most part sunk. It’s become tangled like string, knotted like a ball of thread, and matted like rushes and reeds, and it doesn’t escape the 